From: Paul Philippov <paul@themactep.com>
Subject: [PATCH] t20: attach the mainline V4L2 sensor to tx-isp

The recovered T20 driver expects a vendor sensor subdev registered in
the tx-isp-sinfo registry. The JXF23 now probes from the mainline
driver, so the ISP fell back to creating a vendor I2C client at 0x37
and never streamed.

Add a bridge that finds the mainline V4L2 subdev on the I2C bus and
implements the vendor sensor ABI on top of its pad ops and controls,
prefer it in the video-in register path, and keep the sensor's real
bus/address in the registry.

Map the ISP IRQ through the INTC domain. T20_ISP_IRQ is an INTC hwirq;
requesting it as a raw virq lands on a GPIO irqchip line because the
GPIO domains allocate from the same low virq range, so the VIC never
delivers a frame interrupt.

Keep the ISP and CSI clocks enabled for the lifetime of the module.
The streamer can reach STREAMON without the pipeline prepare, which is
what used to open those gates, and clk_disable_unused has already gated
the boot state; without this the VIC runs unclocked and captures
nothing.

Use v4l2_fh in the image tuning device, register the /proc/jz files
with struct proc_ops, create the jz tree under /proc/jz, and forward
the tuning controls to the vendor core handlers directly. 7.x removed
the subdev s_ctrl/g_ctrl ops and rejects a video open without v4l2_fh;
the old file_operations layout leaves the seq_file NULL, the misplaced
proc tree hides the tuning tables, and the empty handler made every
day/night control return -ENOTTY so libimp dereferenced NULL.

Signed-off-by: Paul Philippov <paul@themactep.com>
---
--- a/driver/t20/Kbuild
+++ b/driver/t20/Kbuild
@@ -33,6 +33,7 @@
